P18-V01 direct `decodes` malformed-tail parity and local gate refresh —
2026-10-22 21:08 UTC

Environment: macOS AArch64, Apple M5 Max (18 logical CPUs), Rust 1.85.0.
Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`. Pinned RocketMQ Java
client/source: 5.5.1, commit `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.

Implementation commit: `2c1f0a73 fix(common): match Java decodes partial
batches`. Crosswalk follow-up confirmed that pinned Java
`MessageDecoder.decodes(ByteBuffer, readBody)` has the same null-stop prefix
behavior as `decodesBatch`. Rust adds `decode_many_compatible` for
`isClient=false`, and `client_decodes` / `client_decodes_with_body` now keep
the valid prefix on malformed tails. Strict `decode_many` and
`decode_many_as_client` continue reporting errors. The invoked Java fixture
checks `decodes`, `decodesBatch(isClient=true)`, and
`decodesBatch(isClient=false)` against a valid record followed by one malformed
byte, including prefix count, runtime subtype, and final cursor position. The
fixture SHA-256 is
`2be37d4f72d8e088a7bc52eb77de5d36192d48ba17de849619c02da31d6c2b97`.

